Both @MockBean and @SpyBean are Spring Boot annotations that integrate Mockito with the Spring application context, but they behave very differently.

@MockBean replaces the real bean in the Spring context with a Mockito mock. All methods return default values — null for objects, 0 for numbers, false for booleans — unless you stub them. This is useful when you want to completely control a dependency, like an external HTTP client or a third-party service, and you do not want any real code to run.

@SpyBean wraps the real Spring bean with a Mockito spy. The real methods are called unless you explicitly stub them with doReturn().when(). This is useful when you want to verify interactions with a real service or intercept just one method while letting everything else run normally — for example, wrapping a real repository to verify it was called, without mocking the actual database query.

An important practical point: both annotations cause Spring to create a modified application context. If two test classes use different @MockBean or @SpyBean configurations, Spring cannot reuse the cached context and will reload it for each test class, significantly slowing down your test suite. So use these annotations sparingly in integration tests and prefer pure Mockito annotations (@Mock, @Spy) in unit tests where no Spring context is needed.
